  "lazy_children_trade": {
    "good_trade": true,
    "what_would_make_it_bad": "It becomes a bad trade if getChildren(element) itself has to re-run or re-touch the filesystem/watcher pipeline per expand rather than reading an already-materialized in-memory model — in that case you've just relocated the same expensive scan to a user-visible interaction (spinner-on-click) instead of amortizing it at idle/watch time. It's also bad if operators habitually expand-all (e.g. via a session default or muscle memory), in which case the deferred-cost model degenerates back into eager cost but now paid synchronously mid-interaction with worse perceived latency than a single upfront paint."
  },
  "density_trade": {
    "acceptable": "operator_must_decide",
    "reasoning": "This is not an engineering call. Collapsing 5 independent glyphs plus a colour-coded fraction into one icon + one dimmed description + one hover tooltip is a real information-density regression for a panel whose entire value proposition is 'scan many times a day without clicking anything.' Tooltips require a hover; a panel that is scanned, not read, loses value if its signals require deliberate hovering to surface. The counterargument (badge, TreeView.message, viewsWelcome, multi-marker via composed icon) can recover some of this but not all of it losslessly. Whether the operator is willing to trade 'all signals visible at a glance' for 'cleaner rows, one authoritative icon, everything reachable via tooltip/context menu' is a product decision, not an architecture one, and this plan currently makes that decision implicitly via the mapping table rather than surfacing it explicitly for sign-off.",
